      "cite": "BK4 printed pp.86-87 = book2-3-4.pdf PDF pp.92-93; derived from the 60-wire topology and the p.86 rule SHIFT LEFT ONE BIT whenever the rotary switch dial is turned; after one clockwise revolution D0..D15 back to D0 from 101101 (relay n takes old relay n+1, relay 6 takes 0), relays 1-6 = 011010; relay n (n=1..6): nY on + (nY chain, 6Y/D16, D16/M+), pushbutton n puts + on P_n = {nA,nX,nG,nF}; + on P_n runs nF-coil-nE-indicator lamp n-nC to minus (nC chain to 6C/6-) and pulls relay n in; nH is on + (nH chain to 6H/6+), so nH-nG holds + on P_n and the relay self-latches; output lamp n is across P_n (nA) and minus (nB/n-). Shift: wiper D16 is on +; pad Dk is kL (k=1..6). Relay k in: kL-kK puts + on P_(k-1) (kK/(k-1)F), copying a 1 into relay k-1; relay k out: kL-kN puts + on (k-1)E ((k-1)E/kN), shorting relay k-1's coil against its own nH-nG hold, copying a 0. D7 = 6E copies a 0 into relay 6. 1K, 1N and D0, D8-D15 are unwired, so D1 does nothing and bit 1 is dropped. Clockwise (increasing) pads D2..D7 copy relay 2 into 1 first and relay 6 last, so each relay is read before it is overwritten. A 0-copy makes relay k-1 buzz through output lamp k-1 while the wiper dwells; the settled 0 assumes the relay is out when the wiper breaks, as the rule requires; dial resting on D0 (unwired), pushbuttons up, so every Dk floats and only latched relays carry current",
